# .railwayignore — what `railway up` must never upload.
#
# Why this file exists
# --------------------
# `railway up` uploads the whole working directory as one archive. In GitHub
# Actions the deploy job can inherit a pnpm store from cache (~100k files) in
# the workspace before deploy runs. The upload can grow past Railway's limit
# and fail with HTTP 413 Payload Too Large, which keeps merged work on main
# from reaching production.
#
# `railway up` already honours .gitignore and skips .git and node_modules on its
# own, so every other large directory here (.turbo, .next, dist, coverage) was
# already excluded. .pnpm-store is the one that was not: it is deliberately
# absent from .gitignore, so nothing filtered it.
#
# This file keeps the upload small no matter what a future job or a local
# `railway up` leaves in the directory.
#
# Format: Railway documents .railwayignore as Railway-specific ignore patterns
# honoured in addition to .gitignore (see docs.railway.com/cli/up, "File
# handling"). Patterns below are plain directory names, left unanchored so they
# match at any depth — every workspace package carries its own node_modules,
# .turbo and dist.

# Package manager stores and installed dependencies
.pnpm-store
node_modules

# Build caches and compiled output
.turbo
.next
dist
*.tsbuildinfo

# Test, coverage and browser artefacts
coverage
test-results
playwright-report
.playwright

# Version control metadata (Railway skips this already; stated for clarity)
.git
